  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Justice, Law and Order Sector Strategic Development Plan IV</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Dutch earmarked support to reduce the case backlog of the judiciary, to enhance safety/security refugees, promote transitional justice and to strengthen commercial justice. In addition, our support will be used to improve the infrastructure and services of remand homes (youth and children) and to address gender/SGBV. This application is a follow up of the Dutch&#x2019; non-earmarked contribution to the JLOS SWAp fund 2012/13 &#x2013; 2014/15  (see Bemo 24617) and the earmarked support to the JLOS SWAp Fund 2014/15 &#x2013; 2016/16 (see Bemo 27725).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the whole project period, some major improvements were achieved in the area of case backlog, reducing the overall number of backlog cases from 5782 in 2016 to 2317 in 2021.  The physical access to justice of the population was also greatly improved in the period under review by the construction of 27 new justice centers (where police, public prosecution, courts etc. sit together in one location), of which 10 were constructed by Dutch funding, together covering 87% of all districts (up from 60% om 2016).  Whereas the COVID-lockdowns had a negative impact on the speed of trials, it also sped up the digitalisation and use of technological assistance in the justice chain, for example by using video conferencing for hearings and testimonies in court.   Through Dutch support the sector also invested in making the services more gender-sensitive. This is shown by the fact that at the start of the project, only 22% of JLOS Institutions had a functional gender desk, but by the end this was 100%. There were also special sessions organised to make sure the backlog in SGBV cases was disposed off.</narrative>
  </description>
